Sys: $(amk.sys name) answers one process or host identity from the kernel with no
shell: pid, ppid, uid, uname (the kernel name), arch (the machine), hostname, epoch
(seconds), or uuid (a fresh version 4 string from /dev/urandom). A makefile spends a
shell on each of these at boot, for run ids, host tags and the parent pid; here each is
one call in process. An unknown name is fatal, so a typo is not an empty id. sys joins
.FEATURES, so a makefile can guard on it and keep the shell for a stock make. The rows
and the routine sit apart from the other api builtins, after the eval row and after
amk_func_require, so a series that grows several builtins at once keeps each patch on
its own context.

